1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,510
Picture a food pantry opening tomorrow.

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:05,830
There is rice on the shelf, but hardly any
produce.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,810
A nearby pantry has produce it could
share.

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:12,340
Someone still has to connect those two
facts, arrange

5
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:15,050
the handoff, and find out what actually
arrived.

6
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:21,050
This is Pantry Relay, created by Shivam
Gupta with AI assistance.

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,880
It helps an approved pantry network fill
upcoming

8
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:26,250
food gaps with what its neighbors can
spare.

9
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:29,180
These are fictional pantries, and every

10
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:31,570
number in this demonstration is sample
data.

11
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:35,910
Cedar Grove has produce above the stock it
needs for its own visitors.

12
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:38,630
Eastside needs produce for its next
service.

13
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:41,780
In the Relay Planner, I can see a proposed
transfer

14
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,690
of 120 pounds, with an explanation of why
it fits.

15
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:50,410
The Planner checks reserves, restrictions,
storage,

16
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:54,270
receiving space, use by cutoffs, and
vehicle capacity.

17
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:59,530
Distances are straight line estimates, so
a person still confirms the actual trip.

18
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:02,690
When I reserve the transfer, that stock is
committed.

19
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:06,190
Another coordinator cannot promise those
same pounds again.

20
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:11,550
Next comes the receiving pantry's
acceptance, pickup, and arrival.

21
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:14,500
The app records the handoffs and requires

22
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,610
temperature checks when the food needs
them.

23
00:01:17,140 --> 00:01:19,270
A driver has a simpler delivery view.

24
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:23,210
Coordinators handle acceptance and the
final receiving record.

25
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:26,090
Here is the part that matters.

26
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,370
We sent 120 pounds, but 8 pounds arrived
damaged.

27
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:34,850
I enter 112 pounds accepted and explain
the difference.

28
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:37,410
The report counts 112.

29
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:41,530
The missing 8 remain visible against the
upcoming need.

30
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:46,470
The receiving pantry's food is protected
from being offered straight back out.

31
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:49,950
A failed delivery counts as no food
received.

32
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:53,060
A receipt confirmed after the service
deadline

33
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:55,510
gets no credit for that missed service.

34
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:59,950
The records follow what happened,
including the inconvenient parts.

35
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:04,280
This is a working application with
accounts, separate

36
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:07,450
networks, permissions, and persistent
records.

37
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:11,380
We test stock protection, permissions, and
the complete

38
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:14,350
handoff in automated back end and browser
checks.

39
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:17,650
It runs without paid AI or mapping APIs.

40
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:21,750
The next question is whether it makes a
coordinator's day easier.

41
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:28,290
Our proposed price is $149 per network per
month for up to 10 sites.

42
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,450
That price is a hypothesis.

43
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:34,800
We want to test it with one coordinator
and three pantries, measuring

44
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:39,050
food gaps, staff time, transport effort,
and accepted pounds.

45
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:43,120
Pantry Relay makes food already in the
community easier

46
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,950
to share, with a record of what reached
the next pantry.

47
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:49,850
Our goal is a useful service that
operators choose to keep.
